# Solvers

Basin ships a growing catalogue of solvers, all driven by the same `Executor`
loop. Pick one based on what your problem provides (a gradient? only costs?
residuals?) and whether it is constrained.

Each solver name links to its API page on [docs.rs](https://docs.rs/basin); the
trailing note cites the paper it implements (or the source it ports).

## First-order

- **[`GradientDescent`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/struct.GradientDescent.html)**
  — steepest descent with a pluggable step rule (`Constant`, `Backtracking`,
  `MoreThuente`, or `Wolfe` line searches) and optional heavy-ball momentum
  (Polyak, 1964).
- **[`Sgd`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/struct.Sgd.html)** —
  mini-batch stochastic gradient descent with a constant learning rate and
  optional Polyak (1964) heavy-ball momentum.
- **[`ProjectedGradientDescent`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/struct.ProjectedGradientDescent.html)**
  — gradient descent for box-constrained problems, projecting each step back
  onto the feasible box.
- **[`BrentDerivative`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/struct.BrentDerivative.html)**
  — Brent's method using first derivatives ("dbrent"): 1-D minimization on a
  bracketed interval, the gradient-using sibling of `Brent` / `GoldenSection`
  (uses the sign of `f'` to bracket and secant extrapolation on `f'` to step;
  Brent, 1973, as transcribed in _Numerical Recipes_ §10.3).

## Derivative-free

- **[`NelderMead`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/struct.NelderMead.html)**
  — the classic reflection / expansion / contraction simplex; needs only a
  `CostFunction`. Implements Lagarias et al. (1998); `NelderMead::adaptive()`
  uses the dimension-aware coefficients of Gao & Han (2012), and the projected
  variant follows Luersen & Le Riche (2004).
- **[`Brent`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/struct.Brent.html)** —
  robust 1-D minimization on a bracketed interval (Brent, 1973, as transcribed
  in _Numerical Recipes_ §10.2); also used inside line searches.
- **[`GoldenSection`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/struct.GoldenSection.html)**
  — golden-section search: 1-D minimization on a bracketed interval, the robust
  linearly-converging companion to `Brent` (Kiefer, 1953; _Numerical Recipes_
  §10.1).

## Quasi-Newton

- **[`Bfgs`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/struct.Bfgs.html)** —
  dense quasi-Newton with a full approximate Hessian (Nocedal & Wright, 2006).
- **[`Lbfgs`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/struct.Lbfgs.html)** —
  limited-memory BFGS for larger problems (two-loop recursion, Nocedal &
  Wright Alg. 7.4).
- **[`Lbfgsb`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/struct.Lbfgs.html)** —
  L-BFGS with box bounds; a faithful port of the Nocedal–Zhu L-BFGS-B v3.0
  Fortran source (Byrd, Lu & Nocedal, 1995; Zhu, Byrd, Lu & Nocedal, 1997,
  ACM TOMS Alg. 778).

## Nonlinear least squares

For problems expressed as residuals (`Residual` + `Jacobian`):

- **[`GaussNewton`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/struct.GaussNewton.html)**
  — undamped normal-equations solver (Madsen, Nielsen & Tingleff, 2004, §3.1).
- **[`LevenbergMarquardt`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/struct.LevenbergMarquardt.html)**
  — damped least squares; the workhorse for curve fitting. Marquardt (1963)
  with Nielsen's (1999) smooth damping update and Moré (1978) / MINPACK column
  scaling (Madsen, Nielsen & Tingleff, 2004, §3.2).
- **[`Trf`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/struct.Trf.html)** —
  trust-region reflective: Levenberg–Marquardt with box bounds (Branch,
  Coleman & Li, 1999, affine scaling).

## Global & stochastic

- **[`CmaEs`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/struct.CmaEs.html)** /
  **[`BoundedCmaEs`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/struct.BoundedCmaEs.html)**
  — covariance-matrix adaptation evolution strategy (Hansen, 2016),
  unconstrained and box-bounded; the bounded variant uses Hansen's adaptive
  `BoundPenalty` (the `pycma` default).
- **[`De`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/struct.De.html)** —
  differential evolution (DE/rand/1/bin) over a feasible box (Storn & Price,
  1997).
- **[`RandomSearch`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/struct.RandomSearch.html)**
  — elitist (1+λ) uniform sampling over a box.
- **[`Ssga`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/struct.Ssga.html)** —
  steady-state real-coded genetic algorithm (replace-worst) with BLX-α
  crossover (Eshelman & Schaffer, 1993), negative assortative mating
  (Fernandes & Rosa, 2001) and BGA mutation (Mühlenbein &
  Schlierkamp-Voosen, 1993); the SSGA component of Molina et al. (2010), §4.4.

## Memetic (composed)

These run a local solver inside a global one — an example of Basin's solver
composition primitives:

- **[`CmaInject`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/struct.CmaInject.html)**
  / **[`BoundedCmaInject`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/struct.BoundedCmaInject.html)**
  — CMA-ES with per-generation local polishing of the best individuals,
  re-injected via Hansen's (2011) injection mechanism.
- **[`DeInject`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/struct.DeInject.html)**
  — the DE-flavored sibling of `CmaInject`: differential evolution (Storn &
  Price, 1997) with per-generation top-_k_ local refinement and Hansen-style
  (2011) injection.
- **[`MaLsChCma`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/struct.MaLsChCma.html)**
  — a memetic algorithm with persistent local-search chains and a CMA-ES inner
  (Molina et al., 2010).

## Constrained (composed)

These wrap any gradient inner solver in an outer loop that handles linear
constraints:

- **[`BarrierMethod`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/struct.BarrierMethod.html)**
  — log-barrier interior-point continuation for linear inequality constraints
  (`A x ≤ b`); needs a strictly feasible start (Boyd & Vandenberghe,
  _Convex Optimization_, §11.3, Alg. 11.1).
- **[`AugmentedLagrangianMethod`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/struct.AugmentedLagrangianMethod.html)**
  — quadratic penalty plus multiplier updates for linear equality constraints
  (`A x = b`); tolerates an infeasible start (Nocedal & Wright, §17.3,
  Alg. 17.4, LANCELOT-style).

The pluggable line searches are documented alongside the first-order solvers:
[`Backtracking`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/line_search/struct.Backtracking.html)
(Armijo; Nocedal & Wright §3.1),
[`Wolfe`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/line_search/struct.Wolfe.html)
(strong Wolfe; Nocedal & Wright Alg. 3.5/3.6), and
[`MoreThuente`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/line_search/struct.MoreThuente.html)
(Moré & Thuente, 1994; a port of MINPACK-2's `dcsrch`).

## Backends and constraints

Two design rules shape which solver you can use where:

- **Constraints are first-class and type-checked.** Box bounds live on the
  *problem* (`BoxConstraints`). Handing a constrained problem to an
  unconstrained solver is a compile error, not a runtime surprise.
- **Backends are tiered.** First-order and derivative-free solvers stay generic
  over the parameter type (`Vec<f64>`, nalgebra, ndarray, faer). Linear-algebra
  heavy solvers (the quasi-Newton and least-squares families) require a backend
  that implements the richer math they need — so an unsupported parameter type
  fails to compile rather than at runtime.

The matrix below summarizes support: ✓ means it compiles and runs on that
parameter type, ✗ means it is a compile-time error (the tiering rule above).

| Solver | Family | `Vec<f64>` | nalgebra | ndarray | faer |
| --- | --- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |
| `GradientDescent` | First-order | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| `Sgd` | First-order | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| `ProjectedGradientDescent` | First-order | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| `NelderMead` | Derivative-free | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| `Brent` | Derivative-free | — | — | — | — |
| `GoldenSection` | Derivative-free | — | — | — | — |
| `BrentDerivative` | First-order | — | — | — | — |
| `Bfgs` | Quasi-Newton | ✓ | ✓ | ✗ | ✓ |
| `Lbfgs` | Quasi-Newton | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| `Lbfgsb` | Quasi-Newton | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| `GaussNewton` | Least squares | ✗ | ✓ | ✗ | ✓ |
| `LevenbergMarquardt` | Least squares | ✗ | ✓ | ✗ | ✓ |
| `Trf` | Least squares | ✗ | ✓ | ✗ | ✓ |
| `CmaEs` | Global | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| `BoundedCmaEs` | Global | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| `De` | Global | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| `RandomSearch` | Global | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| `Ssga` | Global | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| `CmaInject` | Memetic | ✗ | ✓ | ✗ | ✓ |
| `BoundedCmaInject` | Memetic | ✗ | ✓ | ✗ | ✓ |
| `DeInject` | Memetic | ✓† | ✓ | ✓† | ✓ |
| `MaLsChCma` | Memetic | ✗ | ✓ | ✗ | ✓ |
| `BarrierMethod` | Constrained | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| `AugmentedLagrangianMethod` | Constrained | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |

`Brent`, `GoldenSection`, and `BrentDerivative` minimize over a scalar `f64`
interval (1-D), so the vector-backend choice does not apply.

† `DeInject` is itself backend-generic, but its effective coverage is the
intersection of `De` and the chosen inner solver: a `LevenbergMarquardt` or
`Lbfgsb` inner narrows it to nalgebra / faer.

Each solver's page on [docs.rs](https://docs.rs/basin) carries a **Backends**
note listing exactly which parameter types it supports.

See [getting started](../getting-started/) for a worked example, or open the
[visualizer](../../visualizer/) to watch several of these solvers converge.
